<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!-- THE PROMISE, AND WHAT IT COSTS.

         This module used to declare no permission at all and export no component at all, and said
         so here as its defining property. Two of those words have changed and the reasoning had to
         change with them rather than quietly staying on the page. What is left is still the
         narrowest surface in the suite, and it is now a surface rather than its absence:

           1. STILL NO INTERNET. Unchanged, and it is the one that mattered most. There is no HTTP
              client on this module's classpath, no sync, no account, no telemetry, and no
              breach-check lookup — not even the "k-anonymous" kind that sends the first five
              characters of a password hash to somebody else's server. Whatever is in the vault has
              no route off the device that this module could take. Note what that means for the two
              features below: the QR scanner decodes in-process with zxing's plain-Java decoder, and
              autofill answers the system directly. Neither is a network feature wearing a hat.

           2. CAMERA, for one moment. Reading the QR code a site shows when it sets up a second
              factor. The seed in that code is the one credential a household cannot reissue without
              a support line, so typing it by hand from the "can't scan the code?" text was the
              old answer and it is still offered — every screen that scans also takes the key as
              text, so declining this permission costs nothing but convenience. Nothing is recorded,
              no image is kept, and the scanner opens only when somebody taps Scan. The container
              already holds this permission for People's partner pairing, so the app's permission
              set is unchanged; what changed is that this module is now one of the two asking.

           3. TWO EXPORTED COMPONENTS, and only the operating system can reach either. The autofill
              service, behind `BIND_AUTOFILL_SERVICE`, and the credential provider service, behind
              `BIND_CREDENTIAL_PROVIDER_SERVICE`. Both of those permissions are held by the platform
              and by nothing installable, so "exported" here does not mean "reachable by another
              app" — it means the system can bind it, which is the only way either kind of service
              can exist at all. Both are inert until the household picks this app for that job in
              system settings, neither answers with a credential that was not earned (the matching
              rules are in `AutofillMatch` and `PasskeyEntries`), and neither can hand over a secret
              while the vault is shut — what they return then is a way in, never an answer.

              Everything else below is still `exported="false"`, including the two screens those
              services authenticate through: the system starts those through PendingIntents this app
              created, which carry this app's identity rather than the caller's. The list of
              exceptions is meant to stay short enough to read in one sitting.

           4. NO BACKUP AGENT, NO AUTO-BACKUP OF ITS OWN. The vault travels in exactly one way — the
              sandbox's archive, as the sealed file, through `SecretsBackupContributor`. It is safe
              there because the key that opens it is a passphrase in somebody's head rather than
              anything the archive or the phone contains. The *device* unlock (the fingerprint
              shortcut) is the opposite: Keystore-wrapped, device-bound, and deliberately excluded
              from the archive, because a convenience that survived onto another phone would not be
              a convenience.

           5. FLAG_SECURE, on every screen this module owns — the main activity, the two screens the
              services authenticate through, and the scanner. The recents screenshot is written to disk by
              the system, and a vault list, a revealed password and a QR code containing a
              second-factor seed are all exactly the picture nobody wants there. The scanner is a
              subclass of the scanning library's activity that exists for that one line; see
              ui/scan/SecureCaptureActivity. -->

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CAMERA" />

    <!-- A phone with no camera can still hold every second factor here by typing the key, so the
         host app must not inherit a hard requirement from a feature with a real fallback. -->
    <uses-feature
        android:name="android.hardware.camera"
        android:required="false" />

    <!-- Secrets is a library module: no app-level attributes here (name/icon/theme/label live on
         the :app host). Only this app's own components are declared for the manifest merger. -->
    <application>

        <!-- Not a launcher, and not exported: the Operations Sandbox home opens this explicitly,
             in-process. `singleTask` so that returning to it never leaves a second, older copy of
             the unlock screen behind the current one. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:excludeFromRecents="false"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Secrets"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" />

        <!-- The autofill service. See point 3 above for what `exported="true"` does and does not
             mean here; the permission on this line is the whole of it. -->
        <service
            android:name=".autofill.SecretsAutofillService"
            android:label="@string/secrets_app_name"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_AUTOFILL_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.autofill.AutofillService" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.autofill"
                android:resource="@xml/secrets_autofill_service" />
        </service>

        <!-- Where an autofill request goes when it needs a person: to unlock the vault, or to pick
             an item for a form nothing was filed under. Not exported — it is reached through a
             PendingIntent this app made. `excludeFromRecents` because it is a step inside somebody
             else's task rather than a place to come back to, and `stateNotNeeded` so a recreation
             does not try to restore a screen that was showing the vault. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".autofill.AutofillAuthActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:stateNotNeeded="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Secrets"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" />

        <!-- Passkeys. The second of the two exported components, on the same terms as the first:
             BIND_CREDENTIAL_PROVIDER_SERVICE is held by the platform and by nothing installable, so
             the operating system can bind this and no app can. It is inert until the household
             picks Secrets as a passkey provider in system settings.

             Android 14 only. A third-party app can hold passkeys *only* through Credential Manager's
             provider API, which does not exist earlier — so this is the one feature in the suite
             with a floor above the app's own minSdk of 26. The declaration is harmless on older
             phones, where nothing ever binds it. -->
        <service
            android:name=".passkey.SecretsCredentialProviderService"
            android:label="@string/secrets_app_name"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_CREDENTIAL_PROVIDER_SERVICE"
            tools:targetApi="34">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.credentials.CredentialProviderService" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.credentials.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/secrets_credential_provider" />
        </service>

        <!-- Where a passkey is actually made or used — the only place a private key is touched, and
             the only one that requires the vault to be open. Not exported: the system starts it
             through a PendingIntent this app created. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".passkey.PasskeyActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:stateNotNeeded="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Secrets"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"
            tools:targetApi="34" />

        <!-- The QR scanner, subclassed from the library's only to add FLAG_SECURE. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".ui.scan.SecureCaptureActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:excludeFromRecents="true"
            android:screenOrientation="fullSensor"
            android:stateNotNeeded="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Secrets" />

    </application>

</manifest>
